SUMMARY:How Far a Note Travels: The Nadhaswaram and a Music Built for Distance
DESCRIPTION:The nadhaswaram is a mangala vadyam (auspicious instrument) is not merely an accompaniment\, but rather an active part of what makes an occasion sacred. This session aims to help listeners hear gamaka\, the sliding ornamentation that gives a raga its identity\, most audible on the nadhaswaram’s fretless pipe. The instrument’s global cousins are featured\, from the Turkish zurna to the Chinese suona. The session closes with Sheikh Chinna Moulana\, the Muslim vidwan from a three-century lineage who became Asthana Vidwan at Srirangam and Tirupati. \nThe nadhaswaram answers the challenge of filling a temple town with music\, outdoors\, with no electricity\, through brute acoustic engineering: a two-and-a-half-foot cone of hardwood with a double reed\, played with circular breathing so the sound never stops. Paired with the tavil\, a drum of comparable force\, the periya melam (big ensemble) was built to carry across a moving procession\, over crowds in open air\, for hours. The families who played it\, Isai Vellalar\, were classified under the Sudra varna\, and considered ritually polluting\, even as no temple ceremony could proceed without their music. When the microphone arrived in the mid-twentieth century\, the acoustic problem the instrument was built to solve simply disappeared. T.N. Rajarathnam Pillai\, the tradition’s greatest player\, responded by redesigning it: a longer body\, a lower pitch\, tone built for carrying power. Discover its history\, tradition and resonant sound this evening\, \nRecordings are drawn from the Archive of Indian Music\, Smithsonian Folkways and other archival sources\, with acknowledgement from the stage. \nBIC’s Listening Room Sessions are an opportunity to come together\, listen to and discover new music\, refine old tastes and share influences\, stories and memories. \nIn collaboration with: \n \nDeep Notes is a platform cultivating listening literacy — curated listening experiences\, contextual music discussion and cultural dialogue. It works from the position that listening is a practice that can be taught\, and that attention is a skill worth building deliberately. \nVenue: Library\n\n\nA Listening Room Session\nRSVPs closed. Thank you for your interest.\n \n*Facilitator*: Goutham Iyengar\n*Event Categories*: Culture\, Development\, Experience\, Music
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/how-far-a-note-travels-the-nadhaswaram-and-a-music-built-for-distance/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Culture,Development,Experience,Music
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Thumbnail_HFANT.jpg
